{ 1. d4  ♘f6  2. c4  g6  3. ♘c3  d6  4. e4  ♗g7  5. h3  O-O  6. ♗e3  e5  7. d5  ♘a6           ♖⨯♗♕⨯♖♔⨯         ♙♙♙⨯⨯♙♗♙         ♘⨯⨯♙⨯♘♙⨯         ⨯⨯⨯♙♙⨯⨯⨯         ⨯⨯♙⨯♙⨯⨯⨯         ⨯⨯♘⨯♗⨯⨯♙         ♙♙⨯⨯⨯♙♙⨯         ♖⨯⨯♕♔♗♘♖     Black intends to put immediate pressure on the e4-pawn, inducing White to play f2-f3 and weaken the dark squares around the white king. However, this weakening is not critical to the evaluation of the variation, so Black often plays 7...a5 first and only then follows with 8...♘a6 . Depending on personal preference and White's next moves, Black may then place the knight on c5 or choose another option, for example rerouting the other knight via ♘f6-d7-c5.  Note that while positions after  8. g4  ♘c5  9. f3  a5 are included in this chapter, the lines with  7...a5 in which White avoids an early f2-f3 are discussed in the next chapter.   7...♘bd7 would normally serve the same purpose as  7...♘a6 , namely to put early pressure on e4. It transposes to the lines examined in this chapter after  8. g4  ♘c5 .   Black players usually prefer ♞a6 over ♞bd7 because, if White chooses the Classical Makogonov with 8. ♘f3 , then in the key variation 8...♘h5  9. g3 , the f7-f5 push is more effective with the e6-square under the bishop's control. Also, there are lines in which the knight remains on a6 and does not rush to c5.}

