{ 1. d4  ♘f6  2. c4  g6  3. ♘c3  ♗g7  4. e4  d6  5. h3  O-O  6. ♗e3  e5  7. d5  a5           ♖♘♗♕⨯♖♔⨯         ⨯♙♙⨯⨯♙♗♙         ⨯⨯⨯♙⨯♘♙⨯         ♙⨯⨯♙♙⨯⨯⨯         ⨯⨯♙⨯♙⨯⨯⨯         ⨯⨯♘⨯♗⨯⨯♙         ♙♙⨯⨯⨯♙♙⨯         ♖⨯⨯♕♔♗♘♖     Black secures the c5-square against a possible b2-b4 before transferring the knight there. On the downside, this gives White time to defend the e4-pawn with ♘g1-e2-g3, without having to play the weakening f2-f3.  Note that the positions in which White does play an early f2-f3 after Black's ♘b8-a6 are discussed in the previous chapter.}

