[Guid "Rc3f551f1baac41babf8e823a87459749"]
[ContentType "Intro"]
[White "Chapter"]
[Black "Introduction"]
[Result "*"]

{It is essential to understand the ideas of the Classical Makogonov Variation, in which White develops the king's knight to f3. In some sidelines, it is more beneficial to refrain from our standard plan with g2-g4 and ♘ge2, and instead transpose to the classical setup. We have mentioned this in the relevant parts of the previous chapters. Here, we summarise those notes and also add some analysis of lines that do not belong directly to our repertoire, but whose ideas are still worth understanding.  Also, if Black chooses an unorthodox move order, knowing these additional ideas will help White find the right plan.  While developing the king's knight to g3 via e2 has the advantage of adding a resource against f7-f5 and speeding up the attack on the kigside, placing the knight on f3 has benefits too.  In particular, White prevents Black's play on the d8-h4 diagonal e.g. ♗g7-f6 to g5 or h4 or even ♕f4 after a preparatory ♘fd7. From f3 the knight can go to d2 and be diverted to the queenside.  We recommend GM Alex Colovic's video, "I Crushed the King's Indian Without Any Counterplay (https://youtu.be/FYi2DnhYPAc)". In the Games section, you will find a few of Colovic's games from his series of ten consecutive wins with White in the Makogonov, played immediately after he added the variation to his repertoire.  We should also note an important nuance of the move order. White may commit to the "classical" lines by developing the knight to f3 on the 6th or even the 5th move. This allows them to defend the e4-pawn with ♘f3-d2 in case of Black mounting an early attack on the pawn via ♘a6(d7)-c5. Alternatively, White may defer the deployment of the king's knight and play ♗c1-e3 before determining the best route for the knight.}

